course: dictionary
lesson: 7398
title: A pocketful
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A pocketful is a noun. It means as much as a pocket can hold. A pocketful of sweets fills one pocket to the top. Pocketful is built from pocket and full, like spoonful and handful.
explain: Read the context: «Sashko's granny gave him a hug and a pocketful of tsukErky.» Hugs are not things you can count in a pocket, so the sentence is telling you about the sweets. The words "a pocketful of" show that the sweets fill a pocket.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: «gets him a hug and as much as a pocket can hold of tsukErky». If the sentence still makes sense, your meaning is right.
fact: A pocketful means as much as a pocket can hold.
fact: The plural is pocketfuls (you may also see pocketsful).
fact: In the rhyme «Sing a Song of Sixpence», there is «a pocketful of rye».
fact: The ending -ful means as much as will fill something, as in spoonful and handful.
fact: «A pocketful of tsukErky» means a pocket full of sweets.
exercise: «He came home with a pocketful of conkers.» What does pocketful mean? | A very small hole in a coat | *As much as a pocket can hold | A pair of trousers | An empty pocket
exercise: Which word ends in the same way as pocketful? | *handful | sing | rye | pocket
exercise: What is the plural of pocketful? | pocketsfull | pocket | pocketfull | *pocketfuls
test: Picture a girl with «a pocketful of conkers». How many conkers does she have? | Ten, always | None at all | Exactly one | *As many as her pocket can hold
test: In which rhyme does «a pocketful of rye» appear? | Humpty Dumpty | Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star | Baa, Baa, Black Sheep | *Sing a Song of Sixpence
exam: Choose the sentence that uses pocketful correctly. | *He carried a pocketful of coins home from the shop | The jar held a pocketful of buttons | The sky was a pocketful of blue | She ran a pocketful of miles
